From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:27:17 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: add the AVM FRITZ!Box 7580

Third board on this platform, second on the GRX550 die, and the board
the die layer was split out for: almost all of it arrives from
seale_avm_grx550.dtsi unchanged, down to the flash map, which is the
FRITZ!Box 7590's to the byte.

Seven things are its own, and they are the whole of what the vendor's
own board file says. Its DSL companion is a VRX318 rather than a VRX518,
so RC2's inbound byte swapping is cleared -- the setting follows the
part, and the FRITZ!Box 7560 carries the same part with the same value.
Its panel has no ambient-brightness carrier on SSO pin 0, dimming from a
discrete light sensor on i2c instead, which nothing here describes. Its
SSO pins 4, 5 and 6 name different lamps. Its shift clock runs at the
slowest rate the divider offers, 3.125 MHz, where every other board in
the family leaves the default standing. Its panel update rate is set
here rather than inherited, for the reason below. All three of its
buttons move. And it mutes the clkout1 pinmux, which needs nothing from
us because this port never muxed clkout1 and the pad comes up as a GPIO.

SSO_CON1.US is why the update rate cannot be inherited. It selects what
clocks a changed output word out to the external shift register, and the
die layer asks for 250 kHz, which is off the top of the driver's
freq_tbl[]. That takes the last branch of sso_gpio_freq_set() and
selects US=GPTC: a general purpose timer outside the SSO block, which
some other driver is expected to have programmed. Nothing in this kernel
programs one, and un-gating the three GPTU modules would not be enough
either -- the timer has to be running, not merely powered. On a board
that does not come up with one already running, the latch never fires
and the word never leaves: the shift register sits at its all-zeros
power-on state, which leaves the sole active-low lamp lit and everything
else dark, while every register reads back exactly as intended. Asking
for 10 Hz lands on freq_tbl[3], below FPID_FREQ_RANK_MAX, which selects
US=FPID: the divided FSC clock, generated inside the SSO block and
dependent on nothing outside it. Those first four freq_tbl[] entries are
the die layer's 8 kHz fpid clock over 4000, 2000, 1000 and 800, so 10 Hz
is the fastest of them, and a tenth of a second from write to photons is
ample for indicator lamps. The die layer keeps US=GPTC, because that is
what its other board is proven under, but it now says out loud what the
setting depends on.

The buttons deserve the warning they carry in the file: dect and wlan
are swapped against the FRITZ!Box 7560 rather than merely renumbered, so
a block copied from either sibling does not fail loudly. It hands
KEY_RFKILL to the DECT button, and holding that for five seconds is
OpenWrt's factory reset.

The LED master enable is stated here rather than in the die layer, on
the vendor's own second thoughts: the 07.29 GPL release puts it in the
GRX550 layer and the 08.25 GPL release deletes it from there and
re-states it per board. 08.25 carries no HW225 board file at all, so a
naive composition from the newer release loses the line and the whole
panel stays dark while every register reads back correct.

Partition offsets are not in any vendor release, for any board of this
family, by design: the vendor NAND node carries no partitions and its
own comment says the boot loader fills them in. The map here is the
layout that boot loader installs, so that a vendor recovery can hand the
kernel back to the original firmware without remapping flash.

Sources: the vendor's hw225.dtsi and vrx318.dtsi from the 07.29 GPL
release for the board data, and the 08.25 GPL release for the shared
layers.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
index 9a0e2a6631a4..93b0e070e79e 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
 dtb-y	+= easy350_anywan.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7560.dtb
+dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7580.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7590.dtb
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7580.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7580.dts
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..57e5c4e7daaf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7580.dts
@@ -0,0 +1,302 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+// Copyright (C) 2026 Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+/*
+ * AVM FRITZ!Box 7580 (HW225), GRX550. Board-specific data only; the SoC is
+ * described in xrx500.dtsi, the board family in seale_avm.dtsi and the die
+ * variant in seale_avm_grx550.dtsi.
+ */
+/dts-v1/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/input/input.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/leds/common.h>
+
+#include "seale_avm_grx550.dtsi"
+
+/ {
+	model = "AVM FRITZ!Box 7580";
+	compatible = "avm,fritz7580", "intel,xrx500";
+
+	/*
+	 * The boot loader carries a table of device-tree blobs keyed by
+	 * hardware sub-revision. Declaring the tag is cosmetic here — the
+	 * loader passes this kernel no device tree and the appended blob is
+	 * parsed unconditionally — but it stops the loader reporting an
+	 * unmatched sub-revision.
+	 */
+	avm-hw-revision {
+		compatible = "avm,avm_hw_revision";
+		revision = "225";
+		subrevision = "1";
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Capped at the top of the uncached window (PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M):
+	 * above it the KSEG1 alias of a page is a register address, so no
+	 * coherent allocation may land there.
+	 */
+	memory@20000000 {
+		device_type = "memory";
+		reg = <0x20000000 0x10000000>;
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * These aliases name the boot, failsafe and upgrade lamps for
+	 * userspace; without them the boot-progress indication is silently
+	 * absent.
+	 */
+	aliases {
+		serial0 = &asc0;
+
+		led-boot = &led_power;
+		led-running = &led_power;
+		led-failsafe = &led_info_red;
+		led-upgrade = &led_info;
+		led-wifi = &led_wlan;
+	};
+
+	chosen {
+		stdout-path = "serial0:115200n8";
+		/*
+		 * Unused while MIPS_CMDLINE_BUILTIN_EXTEND is selected;
+		 * present so a board that needs its own arguments has
+		 * somewhere to put them.
+		 */
+		bootargs = "";
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Every region below must stay under PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M; the
+	 * uncached alias above that address reaches registers, not DRAM. The
+	 * addresses are the vendor's, stated once for the whole GRX550
+	 * family.
+	 */
+	reserved-memory {
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <1>;
+		ranges;
+
+		/*
+		 * Crash persistence, at the address the vendor's own device
+		 * tree uses for it. 1 MiB of the 4 MiB region below _text.
+		 * The zone sizes fill reg exactly; fs/pstore/ram.c fails the
+		 * whole probe if they overflow it.
+		 */
+		ramoops@20100000 {
+			compatible = "ramoops";
+			reg = <0x20100000 0x100000>;
+			record-size = <0x4000>;
+			console-size = <0x80000>;
+			mem-type = <1>;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * The rest of the region seale_avm.dtsi reserves, whose first
+		 * megabyte the crash log above has taken.
+		 */
+		/delete-node/ avm-reserved@20100000;
+
+		avm-reserved@20200000 {
+			reg = <0x20200000 0x300000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Owned by the second CPU's boot loader; no-map so this
+		 * kernel never maps another CPU's memory.
+		 */
+		cpunet@2dfc0000 {
+			reg = <0x2dfc0000 0x40000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		bootcore@2e000000 {
+			reg = <0x2e000000 0x2000000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Fixed-address seed buffer shared with the second CPU;
+		 * reserved no-map so nothing here maps or reads it.
+		 */
+		shm-rng-seed@3dfb0000 {
+			reg = <0x3dfb0000 0x1000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * Toshiba TC58BVG2S0HTA BENAND, 512 MiB. The labels are ABI: sysupgrade
+ * addresses partitions by label, so they may not be renamed. The map is the
+ * one the vendor boot loader installs.
+ */
+&nand0 {
+	partitions {
+		compatible = "fixed-partitions";
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <1>;
+
+		/*
+		 * First-stage boot loader. Read-only: overwriting it bricks
+		 * the device past the point of recovery without an external
+		 * programmer.
+		 */
+		partition@0 {
+			label = "urlader";
+			reg = <0x0 0x100000>;
+			read-only;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * AVM TFFS environment: the only copy of this unit's identity
+		 * (MAC addresses, serial number). Never written from Linux,
+		 * and erasing it cannot be undone.
+		 */
+		partition@100000 {
+			label = "nand-tffs";
+			reg = <0x100000 0x400000>;
+			read-only;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Two 8 MiB kernel slots, named by position: which one is
+		 * active is boot-loader run-time state, so no label may claim
+		 * to be the active one.
+		 */
+		partition@500000 {
+			label = "kernel0";
+			reg = <0x500000 0x800000>;
+		};
+
+		partition@d00000 {
+			label = "kernel1";
+			reg = <0xd00000 0x800000>;
+		};
+
+		/* UBI: rootfs and rootfs_data. Ends at the 512 MiB mark. */
+		partition@1500000 {
+			label = "ubi";
+			reg = <0x1500000 0x1eb00000>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * RC0 and RC1 carry the two QCA9984 radios; RC2 carries a VRX318. Inbound
+ * byte swapping is off for this endpoint. The PCI domain numbers are
+ * ABI-visible: userspace names the radios 0000:01:00.0 and 0001:01:00.0.
+ */
+&pcie2 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,inbound-swap = <0>;
+	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
+	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 16 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+};
+
+/* 3.125 MHz is the shift-clock rate the vendor asks for on this board. */
+&ssogpio {
+	intel,sso-shift-clk-frequency-hz = <3125000>;
+	intel,sso-update-rate-hz = <10>;
+
+	/*
+	 * The child must be named "ssoled": the driver matches on the node
+	 * name. No label property — colour and function compose the
+	 * ABI-visible LED name.
+	 */
+	ssoled {
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <0>;
+
+		/*
+		 * The two halves of the bicolour info lamp, one physical
+		 * lamp: lit together they mix to orange on the chassis.
+		 */
+		led_info: led@2 {
+			reg = <2>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = "info";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led_info_red: led@3 {
+			reg = <3>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_RED>;
+			function = "info";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 3 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led@4 {
+			reg = <4>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = "fon";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 4 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led_wlan: led@5 {
+			reg = <5>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = LED_FUNCTION_WLAN;
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 5 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led@6 {
+			reg = <6>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = "internet";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 6 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Lit as soon as the driver probes. The lamp is already on
+		 * when the kernel starts, and blinking it off mid-boot would
+		 * read as a failed boot. Active low.
+		 */
+		led_power: led@7 {
+			reg = <7>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = LED_FUNCTION_POWER;
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 7 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+			default-state = "on";
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/* Polled: the GPIO banks publish no irq domain. */
+/ {
+	keys {
+		compatible = "gpio-keys-polled";
+		poll-interval = <100>;
+
+		button-dect {
+			label = "dect";
+			linux,code = <KEY_PHONE>;
+			gpios = <&gpio0 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+
+		button-wlan {
+			label = "wlan";
+			linux,code = <KEY_RFKILL>;
+			gpios = <&gpio0 7 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+
+		button-wps {
+			label = "wps";
+			linux,code = <KEY_WPS_BUTTON>;
+			gpios = <&gpio0 1 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * The master LED enable is a plain GPIO, not an SSO pin, and nothing in this
+ * tree consumes it — hence the hog. The whole panel is dark while it is low.
+ */
+&gpio1 {
+	led-enable-hog {
+		gpio-hog;
+		gpios = <11 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		output-high;
+		line-name = "led-enable";
+	};
+};
